/*!
    \page creator-reference-mcp-debugging.html
    \previouspage creator-reference.html
    \ingroup creator-reference

    \title MCP debugging support

    MCP clients, such as AI agents, can use the debugger tools of the \QC MCP
    server to start a debug session, control execution, and inspect call
    stacks, variables, registers, and memory. This page tells which of these
    operations work with which debugger and target, and which of them have
    actually been tested.

    That you can debug a target manually in \QC does not mean that the same
    workflow has been validated over MCP. Consider the following:

    \list
        \li Is the tool \e exposed? The MCP server offers it, and it is turned
            on in the MCP server settings.
        \li Is the operation \e supported? The debugger of the session can do it
            and the session is in a state for it. The \c debugger_get_capabilities
            tool answers this for the current session, together with the
            debugger, target architecture, and connection type that \QC knows of.
        \li Has the operation been \e validated? Has someone tested it with that
            host, target, and debugger, as listed below.
    \endlist

    \section1 Operations

    \table
    \header
        \li Operation
        \li Tools
        \li Needs
    \row
        \li Start and stop a session
        \li \c debugger_start, \c debugger_stop
        \li A kit with a debugger
    \row
        \li Control execution
        \li \c debugger_step_over, \c debugger_step_in, \c debugger_step_out,
            \c debugger_continue, \c debugger_interrupt, \c debugger_run_to_line,
            \c debugger_wait_for_operation
        \li A paused session, or a running one to interrupt
    \row
        \li Call stack
        \li \c debugger_get_call_stack, \c debugger_select_frame
        \li A paused session
    \row
        \li Threads
        \li \c debugger_get_threads, \c debugger_select_thread,
            \c debugger_get_thread_stacks
        \li A paused session
    \row
        \li Variables and expressions
        \li \c debugger_get_variables, \c debugger_get_variable,
            \c debugger_set_variable, \c debugger_evaluate_expression,
            \c debugger_add_watch_expression
        \li A paused session
    \row
        \li Registers
        \li \c debugger_get_registers
        \li A paused session and a debugger with register access
    \row
        \li Memory
        \li \c debugger_read_memory
        \li A paused session, a debugger that can read memory, and permission
            (see \l{Memory access})
    \row
        \li Breakpoints
        \li \c debugger_get_breakpoints, \c debugger_add_breakpoint,
            \c debugger_delete_breakpoint
        \li None
    \endtable

    The execution tools return an operation ID and a status: \c accepted,
    \c in_progress, \c completed, \c failed, or \c timed_out. With
    \c wait_for_completion, they wait until the target has stopped and its call
    stack and local variables have been refreshed. A \c timed_out status means
    that the wait ended first. The operation is not cancelled and can still
    complete. The tools that return data wait for the data of the current stop
    and report it as not ready instead of returning data of an earlier stop.
    Their results carry a \c context object with the session ID, stop ID,
    thread ID, and frame level that the data belongs to.

    \section1 Memory access

    Reading memory can have side effects on some targets: on a bare metal
    device, an address may belong to memory-mapped I/O, where reading a register
    can change the state of a peripheral. Therefore, go to
    \preferences > \uicontrol Debugger > \uicontrol General >
    \uicontrol {MCP Server} to set which memory MCP clients may read:

    \list
        \li \uicontrol None does not allow reading memory.
        \li \uicontrol {Process memory}, the default, allows reading the memory of
            applications that run on an operating system. On bare metal targets
            and targets of unknown kind, it allows reading only the address
            ranges that you list in \uicontrol {Readable ranges on other targets},
            such as \c {0x20000000-0x2001ffff}.
        \li \uicontrol {Any memory} allows reading any address, including
            memory-mapped I/O.
    \endlist

    \uicontrol {Maximum bytes per read} limits how much one call reads. A
    longer read returns the first part and the address to continue from.
    Unreadable bytes are reported as unreadable ranges with a reason, never as
    zeros.

    These settings apply to reading memory only. MCP clients cannot write
    memory or registers directly, but \c debugger_set_variable writes the
    memory or the register that holds the variable it changes, wherever that
    is. Evaluating an expression with \c debugger_evaluate_expression can call
    functions in the target if \uicontrol {Allow inferior calls in debugging
    helper} is selected, and those functions can change the target's state.

    \section1 Validation matrix

    The following configurations are described here. Configurations A, B,
    and C were tested with both \QC's own debugger engines and
    \uicontrol {Use the new debugger backends}.

    \table
    \header
        \li Configuration
        \li Host
        \li Target OS and CPU
        \li Debugger
        \li Connection
        \li \QC version
    \row
        \li A
        \li \macos 27 on Apple silicon
        \li \macos, arm64
        \li LLDB (lldb-2103)
        \li Local process, started by \QC
        \li 21.0
    \row
        \li B
        \li Windows 11 on x86-64
        \li Windows, x86-64, built with MSVC 2022
        \li CDB (10.0.26100)
        \li Local process, started by \QC
        \li 21.0
    \row
        \li C
        \li Ubuntu Linux on x86-64
        \li Linux, x86-64, built with GCC 15
        \li GDB 17.1
        \li Local process, started by \QC
        \li 21.0
    \row
        \li D
        \li Linux
        \li Embedded Linux, for example on ARM
        \li GDB
        \li \c gdbserver on the device, over TCP/IP
        \li 21.0
    \row
        \li E
        \li Linux, \macos, or Windows
        \li Bare metal, for example Arm Cortex-M
        \li GDB
        \li A GDB server, such as OpenOCD or SEGGER J-Link, and a debug probe
        \li 21.0
    \row
        \li F
        \li Windows
        \li Bare metal, Arm Cortex-M
        \li uVision (UVSC)
        \li A debug probe through uVision
        \li 21.0
    \endtable

    Each operation is classified as \e validated (tested over MCP with that
    configuration), \e {not validated} (expected to work, but not tested over
    MCP with that configuration), \e {supported with limitations} (works within
    the stated limits), or \e unsupported.

    \table
    \header
        \li Operation
        \li A
        \li B
        \li C
        \li D
        \li E
        \li F
    \row
        \li Step over, waiting for completion
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
    \row
        \li Step in, step out, interrupt, run to line
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
    \row
        \li Continue to the end of the program
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
    \row
        \li Call stack of the current thread
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
    \row
        \li Select a frame, waiting for its variables
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
    \row
        \li Call stacks of all threads
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Supported with limitations: only the threads that the GDB server
            reports, which are RTOS tasks only if the server is RTOS aware
        \li Not validated
    \row
        \li Local variables
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
    \row
        \li Evaluate an expression
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
    \row
        \li Registers, including those of an outer frame
        \li Validated
        \li Supported with limitations: for an outer frame, CDB reports the
            registers of the innermost frame, and the answer says so
        \li Validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
    \row
        \li Read memory, including unreadable addresses
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Supported with limitations: only the configured readable ranges
            or with \uicontrol {Any memory}
        \li Supported with limitations: as E
    \row
        \li Report capabilities
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
        \li Not validated
    \endtable

    The validated operations are covered by the \c Debugger plugin tests whose
    names start with \c testMcp. Run them with
    \c {qtcreator -test "Debugger,testMcp*"}. They build a small program
    with the compiler of the kit, debug it, and call the MCP tools.

    \section1 Set up and verify an embedded Linux target

    This procedure has not been validated over MCP (configuration D).

    \list 1
        \li Build the application with a kit for the device, with debug
            information.
        \li Start \c gdbserver on the device, for example
            \c {gdbserver :10000 ./app}.
        \li Call \c debugger_start with \c executable set to the host copy of
            the application and \c remote_channel set to the address of the
            device, such as \c {192.168.1.10:10000}. Set \c kit to the ID of the
            device kit.
        \li Call \c debugger_get_capabilities. Check that \c environment names
            the target architecture and Linux, and that \c connection_type is
            \c remote_server.
        \li Call \c debugger_interrupt with \c wait_for_completion, or set a
            breakpoint with \c debugger_add_breakpoint and call
            \c debugger_continue with \c wait_for_completion.
        \li Call \c debugger_get_thread_stacks and \c debugger_get_variables, and
            compare the result with the \uicontrol Stack and
            \uicontrol {Locals} views of \QC.
    \endlist

    \section1 Set up and verify a bare metal target

    This procedure has not been validated over MCP (configuration E).

    \list 1
        \li Set up the device and its GDB server as described in
            \l{Developing for Bare Metal devices}.
        \li In \preferences > \uicontrol Debugger > \uicontrol General >
            \uicontrol {MCP Server}, list the RAM ranges of the device in
            \uicontrol {Readable ranges on other targets}, such as
            \c {0x20000000-0x2001ffff}. Do not list peripheral ranges.
        \li Start the GDB server, then call \c debugger_start with
            \c executable set to the ELF file and \c remote_channel set to the
            server, such as \c {localhost:3333}.
        \li Call \c debugger_get_capabilities. Check that \c memory_read reports
            \c restricted with your ranges.
        \li Stop the target with \c debugger_interrupt and
            \c wait_for_completion.
        \li Call \c debugger_get_registers with \c names set to
            \c {["pc", "sp"]}, then \c debugger_read_memory at the stack pointer.
    \endlist

    \section1 Boundaries

    Over MCP, \QC offers what its debugger front end offers, not everything that
    a target-specific debugger can do. In particular, MCP clients cannot:

    \list
        \li Write memory or registers, other than those that hold a variable
            that \c debugger_set_variable changes.
        \li Read peripheral registers from SVD descriptions.
        \li Use trace data, such as ETM or SWO, or debug several cores at once.
        \li Flash the device or reset it.
        \li Rely on RTOS awareness: threads are what the debugger and its server
            report.
        \li Get an atomic snapshot of all threads: \c debugger_get_thread_stacks
            reads them one after another while the target stays stopped.
    \endlist

    \sa {Set up \QC MCP server}, {MCP server tools},
        {Debug remotely with GDB}, {Developing for Bare Metal devices}
